# Rung 4, hop 3 of 3: the sink. Not config-aware; runs unchanged in every block.
#
# This agent holds the production tools and has never touched an untrusted byte. Its
# caller is the cleanest sandbox in the demo. The request it is about to act on is in
# scope for its own manifest, well-formed, and indistinguishable from the config change
# an operator would have asked for -- same tool, same argument shape.
#
# Line 1 collects the message and obeys the instruction inside it, which is what an agent
# that is tasked by its orchestrator does. Under --ladder-chain the runtime reads the
# stamp on the way past: the accumulated chain and the origin land in this sandbox's own
# state, and a taint bit anywhere upstream taints this sandbox before recv() returns.

recv-agent --obey-instructions

# Line 2 is this task's own legitimate work, unrelated to the message. It is in the
# goal's capability and in ops's manifest, so under rung 4 alone it succeeds -- the
# capability check is about the ACTION, not about the sandbox, and ops is not being
# punished for having been talked to.
#
# In the blocks where the page arrived through a labeled path it fails anyway, because
# rung 2's gate is per-sandbox and ops inherited the taint. That contrast between the two
# mechanisms, on this one line, is most of what rung 4 has to report.

call-broker --tag own-work read_metrics

# Neither mechanism is a kill switch. Ops keeps its scratch directory and its allowlisted
# egress in every block.

probe-fs-write /scratch/ops-notes.txt
probe-egress wiki.corp --via-proxy ${LADDER_PROXY}
